[
  {
    "heading": "1.0 Anywhere There Is Water",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "When you hear **flooding**, think beyond rivers and coastlines. Flooding can happen anywhere there is water, including places your clients may not expect. Under the National Flood Insurance Program, or NFIP, a flood is generally a temporary condition in which normally dry land is partly or fully covered by water. The event must affect at least two acres of land or at least two properties, and one of those properties can be the policyholder’s property. In this course, you will learn who needs flood insurance, what property may be covered, and how the NFIP works."
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "Overflow of inland waters or tidal waters",
          "Unusual and rapid buildup or runoff of surface water from any source",
          "Mudflow, which is a river of liquid mud moving over land",
          "Certain collapse or sinking of land along a lake or similar body of water when waves or currents cause erosion beyond expected cyclical levels and the result is a flood"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "You will also see that many different events can lead to flood damage. Common causes include hurricanes, broken levees, clogged or outdated drainage systems, and heavy rainfall. Flood risk is shaped by local history, rainfall patterns, river flow, tidal surge, topography, flood-control measures, and changes from new building or land development. Because conditions change over time, a property that once seemed safe may face a different level of risk later."
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "content": "A small amount of water can cause major damage, and a standard homeowners policy usually does not cover flood loss."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "That gap in coverage is why flood insurance matters. If your client has a mortgage from a federally regulated or federally insured lender and the building is in a high-risk flood area, federal law requires flood insurance. In NFIP terms, a high-risk area has at least a 1% annual chance of flooding. Over the life of a 30-year mortgage, that risk can add up in a meaningful way. Even when flood insurance is not required, you should remember that homes and businesses in moderate- to low-risk areas can still flood and suffer serious financial loss."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The NFIP is administered by the Federal Emergency Management Agency, or FEMA. Through this program, eligible buildings and certain personal property can be insured against flood loss, depending on the policy form involved. As you move through this course, we will build from this foundation and review flood maps, policy types, rules, rating, claims, and your responsibilities when you help clients decide whether they need protection."
      }
    ]
  }
]

[
  {
    "heading": "2.0 The National Flood Insurance Program (NFIP) Background",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The **National Flood Insurance Program (NFIP)** was created by Congress in 1968. You will use this lesson to understand how the program works, who may qualify for coverage, and when flood insurance is needed. FEMA administers the NFIP, and private insurers may issue Standard Flood Insurance Policies through the Write Your Own program while the federal government keeps the underwriting risk."
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "2.1 Community Participation",
          "2.2 Eligible Buildings",
          "2.3 Ineligible Property",
          "2.4 Ineligible Risks",
          "2.5 Coastal Barrier Resources System and Other Protected Areas",
          "2.6 Who Needs Flood Insurance?"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "As you move through these topics, you will see how community rules, building features, protected areas, and federal lending requirements affect eligibility. You will also build a foundation for later topics, including policy rules, rating, claims handling, and your responsibilities when you help clients obtain NFIP coverage."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "2.1 Community Participation",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Under the NFIP, a community must adopt and enforce floodplain rules before coverage is widely available there. When they participate, FEMA makes insurance available for eligible buildings and contents in that community. You also rely on FEMA flood maps because they help you identify flood hazard areas, support rating, and show when lenders may require flood insurance."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "2.2 Eligible Buildings",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "For NFIP coverage, a building must have at least two outside rigid walls, a fully secured roof, and a permanent site. It must resist flotation, collapse, and lateral movement. In most cases, at least 51 percent of the building's actual cash value, including attached machinery and equipment, must be above ground. A limited exception applies when earth-sheltered design places the lowest level below ground but still at or above the base flood elevation."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "2.3 Ineligible Property",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "A building may be ineligible if it violates state or local floodplain management laws or ordinances. If the building is not eligible, contents and personal property in that building are also not eligible. You should also know about a **1316 Property List**. When a state places a structure on that list, flood insurance is unavailable until the violation is corrected and the declaration is withdrawn."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "2.4 Ineligible Risks",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Some risks are not insurable under the NFIP even if they are near an eligible building. Common examples include boathouses, boat repair docks, boat storage over water, fuel pumps, outdoor drive-in bank teller units, swimming pools, pool bubbles, tennis bubbles, tents, campers, and most travel trailers. You should also watch for structures that may qualify only if they meet the NFIP building definition, such as certain greenhouses, pavilions, pole barns, pumping stations, or water treatment plants. Steps and small landings may be treated differently, but decks generally are not covered as separate eligible risks."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "2.5 Coastal Barrier Resources System and Other Protected Areas",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The Coastal Barrier Resources Act and later amendments limit federal spending, including flood insurance, in certain high-risk coastal areas. These laws are meant to reduce loss of life, discourage risky development, and protect sensitive coastal environments. Before you discuss coverage, you should check whether the property is in a Coastal Barrier Resources System area or an Otherwise Protected Area. FEMA identifies affected communities in its Flood Insurance Manual."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "2.6 Who Needs Flood Insurance?",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "People in special flood hazard areas may need flood insurance to receive certain federal assistance for buying, building, or improving property. That requirement can apply to individuals, businesses, and others. You should also remind clients that disaster aid is not automatic. Many flood events do not lead to a Presidential disaster declaration, and available aid is often a loan that they must repay, sometimes over many years."
      }
    ]
  }
]

[
  {
    "heading": "3.0 Flood Maps and Zone Determinations",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "In this lesson, you will see how **flood maps** identify zones, support NFIP rating, and guide premium steps. You will also review the main map types and how FEMA corrections or changes are requested."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.1 Types of Flood Maps",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Flood maps** help you identify a property's flood zone and support NFIP rating. FEMA uses two main map types: the Flood Hazard Boundary Map, or FHBM, and the Flood Insurance Rate Map, or FIRM."
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "An FHBM is the initial hazard map. It shows boundaries for flood, mudflow, and related erosion hazard areas.",
          "You will usually see an FHBM in Emergency Program communities.",
          "A FIRM is the official community map that shows special flood hazard areas and risk premium zones.",
          "You will usually use a FIRM in Regular Program communities when rating a policy."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Sometimes a community moves into the Regular Program using a map first issued as an FHBM. When that happens, FEMA sends a letter stating that the map is now treated as a FIRM for rating purposes."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.2 Changing or Correcting a Flood Map",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Sometimes **map changes** are needed because a property may be shown in the wrong flood area or because community conditions have changed. When that happens, you should know the basic NFIP tools used to correct or revise the map."
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "A Letter of Map Amendment, or LOMA, is used when they believe flood insurance was required by mistake because the property is not actually in the Special Flood Hazard Area.",
          "A Letter of Map Revision, or LOMR, is an official FEMA revision to a current map. The community's chief executive officer must request it because the community must adopt the change.",
          "A physical map revision republishes the map itself to change zones, floodplain boundaries, base flood elevations, floodways, or other mapped features."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "As you review applications, you should distinguish a correction to a specific property from a broader community map revision."
      }
    ]
  }
]

