          "how these rules support premium rating, policy administration, and insured responsibilities"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "As you move through these sections, keep in mind that state departments of insurance oversee producer conduct in your state, while FEMA and the NFIP set the flood form rules. When you help clients, you need to identify the building type, occupancy, and ownership interest first. That foundation helps you choose the correct form and explain realistic coverage expectations."
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "content": "Key takeaway: you cannot evaluate flood coverage by property address alone. You must also look at building use, ownership structure, and the specific property or loss involved."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "This lesson also supports later topics on deductibles, waiting periods, cancellation rules, rating steps, and claims handling. If you understand the available policy forms and basic coverage boundaries now, you will be better prepared to explain why a loss is covered, limited, or excluded when they ask questions after a flood."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.1 Dwelling Policy",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The **Dwelling** Policy is used for certain residential buildings and contents. You may use it for a detached one-family home, a two- to four-family non-condominium building, a townhouse or row house, and an individual unit in a residential condominium. The building must stay mainly residential, with only limited incidental business use. You may also write contents-only coverage in some situations, including personal contents kept in a non-residential building."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.2 General Property Policy",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The **General** Property Policy applies when the risk does not fit the Dwelling form. You use it for many other residential and non-residential buildings, such as hotels with short stays, licensed bed-and-breakfast inns, stores, restaurants, schools, houses of worship, warehouses, factories, and farm structures. It also applies to non-residential condominiums, buildings with less than 75 percent residential floor area, detached garages or sheds, and commercial stock or inventory."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.3 Residential Condominium Building Association Policy (RCBAP)",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The **RCBAP** insures a residential condominium building as a whole, including all units within that building. You can use it only in a Regular Program community, and at least 75 percent of the total floor area must be residential. The condominium association carries this policy for the building and common elements. Individual unit owners still may need their own Dwelling Policy if they want coverage for unit improvements or personal contents they own."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.4 Preferred Risk Policy (PRP)",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The **Preferred Risk Policy** was once a separate low-cost NFIP option for eligible properties in lower-risk zones, such as B, C, and X, in Regular Program communities. Since October 1, 2021, you no longer write it as a separate policy form. Instead, its lower-risk residential approach was folded into the Dwelling Policy structure."
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "Residential limits can reach $250,000 for building and $100,000 for contents.",
          "Contents-only coverage may still be available when eligibility rules are met.",
          "Non-residential limits can reach $500,000 for building and $500,000 for contents.",
          "Only one building may be insured on one policy."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "You should also remember that prior flood losses and federal disaster assistance history affect eligibility and pricing considerations. Repeated losses that once blocked PRP eligibility are now reflected through current Dwelling Policy rating rather than a separate PRP form."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "4.5 Flood Policy Definitions",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Several **definitions** shape how you read an NFIP policy. The Act is the National Flood Insurance Act of 1968, as amended. Actual cash value means replacement cost minus depreciation. Replacement cost value means repair or replacement without subtracting depreciation. The application, declarations page, endorsements, and renewal certificate together make up the policy."
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "Base flood: a flood with a 1 percent annual chance.",
          "Basement: an area below ground level on all sides.",
          "Building: a structure with rigid walls and a secure roof, or certain manufactured homes and travel trailers on permanent foundations.",
          "Blanket insurance: not allowed under the NFIP."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "You also need map and elevation terms. A FIRM is the official FEMA map showing Special Flood Hazard Areas, base flood elevations, and rating zones. The lowest floor usually includes the lowest enclosed area, including a basement, but a compliant enclosure used only for parking, access, or storage may be treated differently. Lowest floor elevation is the measured height of that floor above the applicable datum."
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "Emergency Program: early phase of community participation with limited coverage.",
          "Regular Program: later phase with a FIRM in effect and full limits available.",
          "Pre-FIRM and Post-FIRM: building age categories tied to the first effective map date.",
          "Submit-for-Rate: used when no published NFIP rate exists for the risk."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Other terms affect servicing and claims. A binder is not recognized by the NFIP, although renewal certificates are recognized in certain situations. Cancellation ends coverage before expiration. Direct physical loss by or from flood requires actual physical change to insured property. Mudflow can qualify as flood, but landslide, sinkhole, and similar earth movement do not. A valued policy is not how the NFIP works."
