[
  {
    "heading": "5.0 NFIP General Rules",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "In this lesson, you will review the **general rules** that shape how National Flood Insurance Program policies work after a policy is selected and before, during, and after a loss. You will look at deductibles, loss settlement, waiting periods, policy terms, coverage changes, and cancellations. You will also connect these rules to your work with clients, because the way you explain timing, limits, and documentation can affect what they expect and what they recover."
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "You will compare how deductibles apply to building and contents coverage and how deductible choices can affect premium.",
          "You will review the main loss settlement methods, including replacement cost, special loss settlement, and actual cash value.",
          "You will see how the RCBAP, or Residential Condominium Building Association Policy, has its own coinsurance rule for building coverage.",
          "You will learn when coverage may be reduced or reformed if the premium paid does not support the requested amount of insurance.",
          "You will examine the standard waiting period, key exceptions, the one-year policy term, and common cancellation reasons."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "This lesson also supports your broader NFIP duties. You need to match the policy form to the risk, explain when coverage starts, and help clients understand that not every item or situation is covered the same way. For example, a condominium association generally uses an RCBAP for the building, while an individual unit owner commonly uses a Dwelling Policy for unit-related needs. A General Property Policy is typically used for other eligible nonresidential or larger residential risks, not for that unit-owner role."
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "content": "When you discuss NFIP rules with clients, focus on timing, eligibility, documentation, and the exact policy form in use. Small errors in rating data, deductible selection, or effective date assumptions can change coverage or claim payment."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "You will also tie these rules to claims handling. Under the Standard Flood Insurance Policy, covered property can include direct damage to the insured building, but many outdoor items and property outside a fully enclosed building are not covered. As you move through the lesson, keep in mind that state departments of insurance may oversee agent conduct, while the NFIP rules control policy administration and claim handling. By the end, you should be able to explain these general rules clearly and help clients avoid preventable problems."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "5.1 Deductibles",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "A **deductible** is the part of a covered flood loss the policyholder pays before insurance responds. In the NFIP, the minimum deductible can vary based on occupancy, whether the building is Pre-FIRM or Post-FIRM, whether a statutory discount applies, and how much building coverage is purchased. In many cases, if you choose a higher deductible, the premium may go down. For contents-only coverage, the minimum deductible is $1,000."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "You may see separate deductible choices for building and contents coverage. Single-family homes, residential manufactured or mobile homes, residential units, and two-to-four family buildings can use different combinations for building and contents deductibles. Other occupancies use fixed deductible combinations. A policyholder may increase a deductible during the term, and the change usually takes effect when the insurer receives the endorsement request. A deductible may be lowered midterm only in limited cases, such as a mortgagee requiring it or a qualifying rating transition."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "5.2 Loss Settlement",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Loss settlement** explains how the NFIP calculates payment after a covered flood loss. You will see three approaches: replacement cost, special loss settlement, and actual cash value. The method used depends on the type of property, how the building is used, whether it is the insured’s principal residence, and whether enough insurance was carried at the time of loss. When you explain claims to clients, this is one of the most important topics because the settlement method can change the amount paid."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Replacement cost applies to certain single-family dwellings. To qualify, the dwelling must be the insured’s principal residence, meaning the insured or spouse lived there at least 80 percent of the required period before the loss. In addition, the amount of insurance on the dwelling must be at least 80 percent of the building’s full replacement cost right before the loss, or it must equal the maximum building coverage available under the NFIP. If those conditions are met, the policy pays to repair or replace the damaged part of the dwelling after the deductible, without subtracting depreciation, subject to the policy limit and the amount actually spent."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Even under replacement cost, payment has limits. The insurer will not pay more than the building limit shown on the declarations page, the cost to repair or replace the damaged part with like kind and quality for like use, or the amount actually spent to complete the work. If the dwelling is rebuilt at a new location, payment is limited to what it would have cost to rebuild at the former location. Also, when repair or replacement costs are more than $1,000 or more than 5 percent of the amount of insurance on the dwelling, full replacement cost payment is generally not owed until the repair or replacement is completed."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "A policyholder may first claim on an actual cash value basis and later seek the additional replacement cost amount. To do that, the policyholder must notify the insurer within 180 days after the date of loss. This rule matters when repairs cannot begin right away. You should explain that actual cash value means the value of the damaged property with depreciation taken into account, while replacement cost does not deduct for depreciation if the conditions are met."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Special loss settlement applies to certain manufactured or mobile homes and travel trailers used as single-family dwellings. The structure must meet size requirements and must be the insured’s principal residence. If it is totally destroyed, or damaged so badly that the insurer decides it is not economically feasible to repair to its pre-damage condition, the insurer may pay the lesser of the replacement cost or one and one-half times actual cash value, or the building limit shown on the declarations page. If the damage is partial and repair is economically feasible, the loss is settled under the replacement cost rules instead."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Actual cash value, or ACV, applies when replacement cost or special loss settlement does not apply. It also applies to several categories of property even when the building itself may qualify for a different method. If a single-family dwelling does not carry enough insurance to satisfy the replacement cost requirement, the payment may be based on ACV or on a proportionate share of repair or replacement cost, depending on how much insurance was carried compared with the amount required. This is why accurate valuation and correct coverage selection matter so much when you help clients apply."
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "Two-, three-, and four-family dwellings",
          "Units not used only for single-family dwelling purposes",
          "Detached garages",
          "Personal property",
          "Appliances, carpets, and carpet pads",
          "Outdoor awnings, antennas, aerials, and similar outdoor equipment",
          "Debris from abandoned covered property left at the described location",
          "A dwelling that is not the insured’s principal residence"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "content": "You should never assume a flood claim will be paid on a replacement cost basis. The property type, occupancy, principal residence test, and amount of insurance carried all affect the final settlement method."
      },
      {
        "type": "knowledge_check",
        "question": "Which situation best fits NFIP replacement cost loss settlement for a dwelling?",
        "options": [
          "A) A non-owner-occupied vacation home with limited building coverage",
          "B) A single-family principal residence insured to at least 80 percent of replacement cost or the NFIP maximum",
          "C) A detached garage with building coverage",
          "D) A two-family dwelling with contents coverage only"
        ],
        "correct_answer": "B",
        "explanation": "B is correct because replacement cost generally applies to a single-family dwelling that is the insured’s principal residence and carries enough insurance to meet the 80 percent or NFIP maximum requirement. A is incorrect because a vacation home is not the principal residence. C is incorrect because detached garages are settled on an actual cash value basis. D is incorrect because a two-family dwelling does not meet the single-family replacement cost rule described here, and contents-only coverage does not create replacement cost eligibility for the dwelling."
