    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The Coastal Barrier Resources Act and later amendments limit federal support, including flood insurance, for development in certain high-risk coastal areas. These laws aim to reduce loss of life, avoid wasteful federal spending, and protect sensitive coastal environments. Before you discuss coverage, you should check whether the property is in a Coastal Barrier Resources System area or an Otherwise Protected Area. FEMA identifies affected communities in the Flood Insurance Manual."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "2.6 Who Needs Flood Insurance?",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Under the Flood Disaster Protection Act of 1973, people or businesses in special flood hazard areas may need flood insurance if they want certain federal assistance for buying, building, or improving property. You should also remember that disaster aid is not the same as insurance. Many flooding events never receive a Presidential disaster declaration, and much available aid is a loan that they must repay, sometimes over many years with interest."
