        "content": "To qualify, a building generally must have at least two outside rigid walls, a fully secured roof, and a permanent site. It must resist flotation, collapse, and lateral movement. Usually, at least 51% of the building’s actual cash value, including attached machinery and equipment, must be above ground. A limited exception applies when an energy-efficient design places a level below ground but still at or above the base flood elevation."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "2.3 Ineligible Property",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Some property cannot be insured under the NFIP. If a building violates state or local floodplain management laws or ordinances, that building and its contents may be ineligible. Under Section 1316 of the National Flood Insurance Act, a state can place a structure on a 1316 property list. If the violation is corrected and the declaration is withdrawn, flood insurance can become available again."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "2.4 Ineligible Risks",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
