    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The **National Flood Insurance Program (NFIP)** was created by Congress in 1968. It gives you a framework for flood coverage through FEMA and participating private insurers. In this lesson, you will learn how community participation supports coverage, how flood maps and zones guide eligibility and rating, which buildings can qualify, which property and risks cannot, how protected coastal areas affect availability, and who must carry flood insurance. You will also see how the Write Your Own program lets insurers issue Standard Flood Insurance Policies in their own names while the federal government keeps the underwriting risk. As you work with clients, these background rules help you explain why coverage may be available in one situation and unavailable in another. State insurance regulators oversee insurance activity in their jurisdictions, but NFIP eligibility and policy rules come from FEMA."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "2.1 Community Participation",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "For NFIP coverage to be available, a community must adopt and enforce floodplain rules for flood-prone land. In return, FEMA makes coverage available for buildings and contents in that community. Flood maps help you identify hazard areas, support rating, and show lenders when purchase requirements may apply."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "2.2 Eligible Buildings",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "To qualify, a building must have at least two rigid outside walls, a fully secured roof, and a permanent site. It must resist flotation, collapse, and lateral movement. Usually, at least 51% of the building's actual cash value, including attached machinery and equipment, must be above ground, with a limited energy-efficient construction exception."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "2.3 Ineligible Property",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "A building may be ineligible if it violates state or local floodplain management laws or ordinances. If the structure is ineligible, its contents and personal property also cannot be covered. Under Section 1316 of the National Flood Insurance Act, a state may place a violating structure on a 1316 Property List until the violation is corrected."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "2.4 Ineligible Risks",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Some risks are not eligible even when you are working in a participating community. Common examples include boathouses, campers, fuel pumps, open stadiums, tents, swimming pools, pool bubbles, tennis bubbles, and storage tanks. Travel trailers are also ineligible unless they are permanently converted and meet local permit rules. Some items, such as greenhouses, pole barns, pavilions, pumping stations, and water treatment plants, may qualify only if they meet the NFIP building standards."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "2.5 Coastal Barrier Resources System and Other Protected Areas",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The Coastal Barrier Resources Act and later amendments limit federal financial assistance, including flood insurance, for certain residential and commercial development in designated high-risk coastal areas. These protected areas include the Coastal Barrier Resources System and Otherwise Protected Areas. Before you discuss coverage, you should check FEMA resources and the Flood Insurance Manual to confirm whether the property is in one of these restricted areas."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "2.6 Who Needs Flood Insurance?",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "If a property is in a special flood hazard area, individuals, businesses, and others who buy, build, or improve property generally must obtain flood insurance to receive federal assistance. You should also remember that disaster aid is not automatic. Many events never receive a Presidential disaster declaration, and much assistance comes as a loan that they must repay, often with interest over many years."
      }
    ]
  }
