        "content": "A building may be **ineligible property** if it violates state or local floodplain laws or ordinances. If the building is not eligible, contents in that building are also not eligible. Under Section 1316 of the National Flood Insurance Act, a state can place a structure on a 1316 Property List. You may write coverage again only after the violation is corrected and the declaration is withdrawn."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "2.4 Ineligible Risks",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Some items are not insurable under the NFIP even if they are near an eligible structure. Common examples include boathouses, boat repair docks, fuel pumps, swimming pools, tents, open stadiums, and storage tanks. Travel trailers are also excluded unless they are permanently converted and meet community permit rules. A greenhouse, pole barn, pavilion, or pumping station may qualify only if it truly meets the NFIP definition of a building. When you review an application, you should confirm that the risk is a qualifying building, not just property exposed to flood."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "2.5 Coastal Barrier Resources System and Other Protected Areas",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The **Coastal Barrier Resources System (CBRS)** and Otherwise Protected Areas limit federal support in certain high-risk coastal locations. These rules were designed to reduce loss of life, avoid wasteful federal spending, and protect sensitive coastal environments. Because federal flood insurance is generally barred for residential or commercial development in these designated areas, you should check FEMA resources and the current Flood Insurance Manual before offering coverage."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "2.6 Who Needs Flood Insurance?",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "If a property is in a special flood hazard area, flood insurance may be required for people or businesses seeking federal assistance to buy, build, repair, or improve that property. You should explain that disaster aid is not automatic and often depends on a Presidential declaration. You should also explain that much disaster assistance comes as a loan, such as an SBA disaster home loan, which must be repaid with interest."
