          "Establish the construction date to decide whether the building is Pre-FIRM or Post-FIRM."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Next, you check the community’s program status. A community may be in the Emergency Program or the Regular Program. You also check whether it participates in the Community Rating System, or CRS. CRS is a program that can reduce premiums in communities that go beyond minimum floodplain management standards. As an agent, you should confirm community status through the insurer, local officials, or the NFIP Community Status Book so you do not quote coverage where none is available or miss a discount they may receive."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "You also determine where contents are located inside the building, because contents on a higher floor may face different exposure than contents in a lower enclosed area. Then you determine the date of construction. This step matters because it helps you classify the building as Pre-FIRM or Post-FIRM. In general, the key date is December 31, 1974, or the effective date of the community’s initial Flood Insurance Rate Map, whichever is later. You can find that initial FIRM date in the Community Status Book."
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "content": "For NFIP rating, you base the risk on the property’s actual location, not on a different mailing address."
      },
