    "heading": "1.0 Anywhere There Is Water",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "When you hear the word **flooding**, you should think beyond coastal storms. Flooding can happen anywhere there is water, including inland neighborhoods, small towns, and developed areas with poor drainage. Under the National Flood Insurance Program, or NFIP, flooding generally means a temporary condition where normally dry land is partially or completely covered by water. The event must affect at least two acres of dry land or at least two properties, and one of those properties can be your policyholder’s property."
      },
      {
        "type": "bullet_list",
        "items": [
          "Overflow of inland waters or tidal waters",
          "Unusual and rapid buildup or runoff of surface water from any source",
          "Mudflow, which is a river of liquid and flowing mud on normally dry land",
          "Collapse or sinking of land along a lake or similar body of water when waves or currents erode or undermine the shore during a flood event"
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "In practice, you may see flood losses after hurricanes, heavy rain, broken levees, clogged drainage systems, or older stormwater systems that cannot handle runoff. A small amount of water can cause major damage to floors, walls, wiring, and personal property. You should also remember that flood risk is not based on one fact alone. State insurance regulators and FEMA materials point to several factors, including loss history, rainfall patterns, river flow, tidal surge, land shape, flood-control systems, and changes caused by building and development."
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "content": "You cannot assume a homeowners policy covers flood damage. In most cases, standard homeowners insurance does not cover flooding, so your client may need separate flood insurance."
      },
